{
  "summary": "Adds explicit destructors to CHMAC_SHA256 and CHMAC_SHA512 to wipe their inner and outer hash states using memory_cleanse(). In CreateMuSig2PartialSig(), replaces stack-allocated secp256k1_keypair structures with KeyPair via CKey::ComputeKeyPair(), ensuring the private key material is kept in secure memory and cleansed on destruction.",
  "problem": "CHMAC_SHA256 and CHMAC_SHA512 leave internal hasher states in memory upon destruction, preserving HMAC output and trailing message bytes from sensitive operations such as CExtKey::SetSeed() (seed and master key) and hardened BIP32 derivation (parent private key). Additionally, CreateMuSig2PartialSig() instantiates secp256k1_keypair on the stack without zeroing it on function exit.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author note in PR description: 'I checked with a small program, built from these files, that the seed and key bytes are in the HMAC object after Finalize() and gone after its destructor.'",
    "Continues memory cleansing work from merged PR #35254 ('crypto: cleanse HMAC stack buffers after use and ChainCode')."
  ],
